00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.910 align:start position:0%
 
all the kind of details that we can chew  over now with the shadow Chancellor  labors Rachel Reeves a very good morning  to you thanks for coming in so what did  you make of it  I think there's two key things that I  would have done differently and  approached differently uh yesterday the  first is around Fair taxes and you've  just set out some of the tax changes  yesterday but some of the things that  weren't in there for example the non-dom  tax status still exists if we close that  loophole that would bring people in to  pay tax if you make Britain your home  you should pay your taxes here it could  bring in more than three billion pounds  a year private Equity bosses their  income is not taxed at the same rate  that ordinary working people's income is  is taxed that's another loophole that  should have been closed yesterday and  instead of just coming time and time  again to the ordinary working man and  woman asking them to pay more in taxes I  would like to see more from those on the  broader shoulders that the second thing  that it was really frustrating not to  see yesterday was a real plan for  economic growth because ultimately that  is the way to lift living standards and  despite a lot of the Doom and Gloom I'm  really ambitious about our country's  future there are Industries where we  have real strengths from some of the  green industries of the future to Life  Sciences to data and fintech and there  wasn't a plan yesterday to really make  the most of our potential as a country  okay and I'll come on to latest plan in  a moment but but first you talk about  your concerns about this but there's a  lot here for you to like isn't there I  mean more higher earners could be paying  more more tax with those shifting income  tax thresholds poorer people largely  protected there's a rise in the living  wage working age benefits rising in line  with inflation there's a lot that labor  should like here isn't there  I didn't feel like that I must say I'm  really worried about what's going to  happen to people's living standards uh  next year from April big increases in  gas and electricity bills whilst the  windfalls of War being made by the  energy Giants are still not taxed to the  degree that they they should be in every  pound left on the table 10 percent  there's still those windfalls of of war  that we could be taxing more we should  not be having these investment  allowances uh which it just means that  there are some big energy companies that  aren't even paying any of this windfall  tax that's not right and and so we would  have done more on that front to  alleviate some of that pressure on the  ordinary working person who you know at  the moment you you get your income is  being eroded by double-digit inflation  and now it's going to be eroded further  by these range of stealth taxes that are  coming in and we know that bills are  going to go up further next year so I  understand that people feel really  anxious about what's happening at the  moment and when you see some of the you  know big companies in the very  wealthiest in society you know getting  off scot-free I I just don't think  that's right it's not the right  priorities well the chancellor insists  is that it's the better off that are  suffering the most here well not if  you're a non-dorm not if you're a  private Equity boss not if you're a  banker he would say but not but the  point it is that if you're a non-dom  you're not paying your taxes in Britain  so it doesn't matter whether the rate  kicks in at 125 Grand or 150 Grand  you're not paying it if you're a non-dom  that is the whole point and so we should  be closing those loopholes and again if  you're a private Equity boss you're not  paying income tax you say it's all a  capital gain uh and and that's taxed at  a lower rate so that's why we've got to  close some of these loopholes to make  our tax system uh fairer okay let's see  what labor would do shall we because the  chancellor is announced tax Rises now  and spending cuts for later two years  later when it's possible that labor  would be in power so would you stick to  those spending cards well first of all I  do recognize that because of the mess  that the Tories have made labor are not  going to be able to do everything that  we want to do as quickly as possible and  we've set a set of fiscal rules at an  incoming labor government would stick to  but again there are different choices  that could be made different choices  could have been made yesterday if the  government don't make those choices  those are the sorts of different choices  that we would be making around tax for  example at the next election I'd also  say this you know in the last year we've  had four budgets and four chancellors by  the next election we may have another  four budgets another eight budgets who  knows so I don't know the state of the  um of the fiscal position I'm going to  would you stick to those spending cuts  well I have said that we've got a set of  fiscal rules to balance day-to-day  spending to get debt down and we will  set out our our  particular our specific proposals at the  next election but I do recognize that  because of the mess they've made that  does impose constraints on an incoming  labor government but we would make  different choices uh in terms of tax to  alleviate some of that pressure on the  ordinary working person and also we were  put in plan our plans to grow the  economy because ultimately that is the  way okay living but I wanna I wanna I  know you can't talk absolute specifics I  want a general idea of what you're going  to do as far as the spending is  concerned if you do I mean do you want  more money spent on public services well  I've said for example example on this  non-dom tax you know that we would use  that money by closing that loophole to  invest in the NHS Workforce but how much  money would that rate 3.2 billion pounds  but that's not a huge amount when the  whole budget is 136 billion the  government aren't doing it and that is  the whole point we could use that to  double the number of people going to  medical training school I say that's a  massive big deal to increase the number  of nurse training places Midwife  training places District nurses those  are just some of the differences so even  if you've got a different a difficult  fiscal inheritance and we know that a  labor government will have that because  the mess notorious have made you can  still make different choices you can  prioritize different things and under  labor it would be ordinary working  people that were prioritized and  ensuring that our public services are  lifted off of their knees which is where  they are at the moment and asking those  with the broader shoulders to contribute  more if you do want to spend more on  public services  a lot of the ideas have already been  used by the chancellor haven't they that  labor has talked about in the past like  the windfall tax on energy companies  like higher taxes for higher earners I  mean you don't have many options left at  you to raise the money well I've set out  some of them private Equity uh VAT on  private school fees uh the non-dom tax  status a proper windfall tax so that  those windfalls of War are used to help  people who are really struggling with  higher bills and higher taxes under the  conservative governments those are some  of the different choices that we would  make now if the tourists don't make  those choices they're the choices that  we would make after the next election  with the labor government do you back  the fact that Jeremy Hunt has honored  the triple lock on pensions and that'll  see pensions rise in line with inflation  it is right that the chancellor  increased the pensions and Universal  Credit and disability benefits in line  with the rising cost of living I'm still  concerned about what happens next year  when energy bills go up to 3 000 pounds  on average for for people and how people  are going to cope with that  um but I am pleased that those choices  were made because that's caused a huge  amount of uncertainty and worry for some  of the most vulnerable people in our  society and yet are you concerned that  while pensioners are protected a lot of  workers haven't been are you comfortable  with that and if you're not what kind of  pay Rises do you want to see the public  sector get to to match anything like  what pensioners are getting well I want  to see inflation brought under control  and and that means bringing back  stability to our economy and our economy  has not had that stability with wild  volatility in in in guilt prices and  yields  we haven't got very much time but should  public sector workers get anything like  pensioners  settlements I'm not going to plot  numbers out of thin air but what we do  need is the health secretary and other  government ministers treating public  sector workers with respect getting down  and negotiating this is the first time  ever that nurses have gone on strike  this should be a badge of Shame for this  government they can still avert it if  they take those concerns of those who  work in our NHS heroes in our country if  they show them the respect and treat  them with the dignity that they deserve  okay Rachel Ruiz thanks very much for  coming in thank you

